The attached image (Figure 6) illustrates how Nano-11 improves antigen targeting to immunopotentiating cells and enhances its retention at the injection site, supporting Nano-11's role as an effective vaccine adjuvant. Panel a shows that OVA-positive migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) in the draining lymph node exhibit higher CD86 expression, indicating their enhanced activation potential compared to other antigen-presenting cells. Panel b further characterizes OVA+ and OVA- MigDCs based on CD11b and Ly6C markers, revealing the specific DC subtypes involved in antigen uptake. Panel c demonstrates in vivo imaging results, where Alexa Fluor 647-labeled OVA, when formulated with Nano-11, is retained at the injection site for a longer duration (up to 7 days) compared to soluble OVA or OVA formulated with aluminum hydroxide.